    "case_id": "729",
    "case_summary": "A 79-year-old female patient presented with severe chest pain lasting for 4 hours. Medical history included hypertension (moderately managed), paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (treated with Betaloc for 6 months), and moderate pulmonary hypertension (pulmonary artery systolic pressure ~55 mmHg). The patient had no history of diabetes or hyperlipidemia.\n\nInitial vital signs during procedure showed low blood pressure of 60/40 mmHg, which improved to 90/50 mmHg with heart rate of 96 beats/min after intervention. About 30 minutes post-procedure, patient experienced severe chest pain radiating to the back, with dyspnea and sweating. Vital signs showed blood pressure 100-111/54-70 mmHg, SpO2 84%, and heart rate 110-142 beats/min.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  • cTnT: 0.309 ng/ml\n  • CK-MB: 55 U/L\n  • D-Dimer: within normal range\n\n- ECG Findings:\n  • Initial ECG showed ST-segment elevation in inferior-posterior leads\n  • Post-procedure ECG showed mild ST elevation in leads II, III and AVF; mild ST depression in leads V2 and V4\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  1. Coronary Angiography:\n     Title: Emergency Coronary Angiography\n     Description: Revealed occlusive thrombosis of the tiny distal left circumflex artery (LCx), with no notable lesions in remaining coronary vessels\n\n  2. Computed Tomography Angiography (CTA):\n     Title: Thoracic and Abdominal Aorta CTA\n     Description: Revealed pericardial effusion and contrast leakage from myocardium to pericardial cavity\n\n  3. Bedside Echocardiography:\n     Description: Showed fluid accumulation within pericardial cavity (10mm anterior to right ventricular anterior wall, 11mm posterior to left ventricular posterior wall, 15mm lateral to left ventricular lateral wall, and 15mm at apex)",
    "frontier": [
